        <p class="lead"><strong>You can combine JPG, PNG, and WebP images into a single PDF, and the Best Answer Hub Images to PDF Converter does it entirely in your browser so your photos are never uploaded.</strong> This guide explains how an image actually becomes a PDF page, whether the conversion loses quality, why iPhone HEIC photos need one extra step, the hidden GPS location your photos can carry, and why uploading a scanned ID or bank statement to a random converter is a risk worth avoiding.</p>

        <div class="cav">Start here</div>
        <h2 id="what">What is the Best Answer Hub Images to PDF Converter?</h2>
        <p>The Best Answer Hub <a href="/tools/images-to-pdf/">Images to PDF Converter</a> turns a set of JPG, PNG, and WebP images into one PDF, with drag-and-drop reordering, a choice of page size, orientation, margins, and how each image fits the page. It is built on the open-source pdf-lib library, which assembles the PDF in your browser using JavaScript, so nothing is transmitted, nothing is stored, and it keeps working with the internet switched off. There is no account, no watermark, and no daily cap. The tool sits in the Best Answer Hub <a href="/pdf-toolkit/">PDF Toolkit</a> and the wider <a href="/tools/">Tools hub</a>, is built and maintained by <a href="/about/shahbaz-ali-malik/">Shahbaz Ali Malik</a>, and stays free because Best Answer Hub is funded by optional paid assessments rather than advertising.</p>

        <div class="cav">The part that matters</div>
        <h2 id="safe">Is it safe to convert an ID or a bank statement online?</h2>
        <p>Converting is safe only when the work happens on your own device, which is exactly how the Best Answer Hub Images to PDF Converter works. The catch with most other converters is that they upload your images to a server first. JPG2PDF states plainly that "our server uploads a copy of your original file and then uses that for the conversion process." Smallpdf keeps uploaded files for one hour, and iLovePDF says files are "permanently deleted within two hours of being processed." Those windows are short, but they still mean a scan of your passport, ID, or bank statement left your device and sat on someone else's server. The safer approach is to not send it at all, which the FTC frames as a first principle: if you do not have a real need to collect sensitive information, "don't even collect it" (<a href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/protecting-personal-information-guide-business" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FTC</a>). The stakes are set by the numbers, with the average United States data breach reaching a record 10.22 million dollars in 2025 (<a href="https://www.ibm.com/reports/data-breach" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IBM</a>).</p>
