        <div class="cav">Under the hood</div>
        <h2 id="how">How does an image become a PDF page?</h2>
        <p>An image becomes a PDF page by being embedded as an object inside the PDF and then placed on a page of a chosen size, and the Best Answer Hub Images to PDF Converter does this with pdf-lib. PDF is an open ISO standard, ISO 32000, and it can carry a JPEG's compressed data directly using a filter called DCTDecode, which the specification describes as a technique "based on the JPEG standard" (<a href="https://opensource.adobe.com/dc-acrobat-sdk-docs/pdfstandards/PDF32000_2008.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ISO 32000-1</a>). That means a JPEG can be stored inside the PDF without being re-compressed, so it does not lose quality on the way in, while a PNG is embedded losslessly. Each image is then placed on a page measured in points, where there are 72 points to an inch, so an A4 page is 595 by 842 points and a US Letter page is 612 by 792 points. The tool lets you pick A4, Letter, or a page sized to match each image.</p>
