        <div class="cav">Keeping it sharp</div>
        <h2 id="quality">Does converting to PDF reduce quality?</h2>
        <p>In most cases it does not, and the Best Answer Hub Images to PDF Converter is built to keep your images intact. A PNG is embedded losslessly, and a JPEG is embedded directly whenever possible, which means its original compressed data is carried into the PDF without a second round of compression. The one time quality can change is if you lower the quality slider below 100 percent, which asks the tool to re-encode a JPEG at a smaller size, a trade you make on purpose to shrink the file. WebP images are converted to PNG, which keeps all their visual data. For the sharpest result, leave the quality at 100 percent; to make a heavy batch smaller, drop it to around 75 to 85 percent, or run the finished PDF through the Best Answer Hub <a href="/tools/pdf-compressor/">PDF Compressor</a>.</p>
        <div class="callout peach"><div class="h">Lossless versus lossy, in one line</div><p>PNG is lossless, so it never degrades. JPEG and WebP are lossy formats, but embedding a JPEG directly adds no new loss. You only re-compress a JPEG if you choose to, by lowering the quality slider to trade a little sharpness for a smaller file.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">The iPhone catch</div>
        <h2 id="heic">Why do my iPhone photos not work?</h2>
        <p>iPhone photos usually will not convert directly because they are saved in HEIC, a format the tool does not accept, and the Best Answer Hub Images to PDF Converter is honest about that rather than failing silently. HEIC, part of the HEIF standard (ISO/IEC 23008-12), has been the default iPhone photo format since iPhone 7 and iOS 11, because it stores photos at high quality in a smaller file. The snag is that browser support for decoding HEIC is inconsistent, so most in-browser tools, including this one, accept JPG, PNG, and WebP instead. The fix is quick: on the iPhone, open Settings, then Camera, then Formats, and choose Most Compatible so new photos save as JPEG. For photos you already have in HEIC, run them through a free HEIC-to-JPEG step first, then convert.</p>

        <div class="cav">The hidden passenger</div>
        <h2 id="metadata">Does the converted PDF still hold my photo location?</h2>
        <p>It can, and the honest answer is worth knowing before you share a PDF. Photos from a phone or camera carry EXIF metadata, defined by the Exif standard (CIPA DC-008), which the US Library of Congress notes can include "geographic data," meaning the GPS coordinates of where the photo was taken, along with the camera model and timestamp (<a href="https://www.loc.gov/preservation/digital/formats/fdd/fdd000618.shtml" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Library of Congress</a>). Privacy advocates have long warned that these coordinates "hidden in these quick and candid pictures" are an easy way to work out where someone was (<a href="https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2012/04/picture-worth-thousand-words-including-your-location" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EFF</a>). Because the Best Answer Hub Images to PDF Converter never uploads your photos, that location never reaches a server either way. If you plan to share the PDF widely, keep in mind that a direct, full-quality embed can carry the original photo's metadata, while re-encoding the image, for example by lowering the quality slider, drops it, since a re-encode keeps only the pixels.</p>
        <div class="callout sage"><div class="h">Before you share a photo PDF</div><p>If the images are personal photos and the PDF is going somewhere public, consider lowering the quality slider a notch, which re-encodes the image and strips its EXIF data, including GPS. For a scan of a document rather than a camera photo, there is usually no location data to worry about in the first place.</p></div>

